ne announces each deny it installs to the session's system prompt, and every Claude session it launches with a run directory also gets the creel trust sentence, through one `append-system-prompt.txt` file named by `--append-system-prompt-file` on launch and resume, so a session knows the tool is unavailable before it tries one and follows `[creel]` mail from its hooks. Every settings document the Claude engine writes, a run's and an opened session's alike, sets Claude Code's `attribution` to empty commit and pull-request texts, so no session lym launches credits itself in a commit or pull request and a landing's credit block stays the one credit. While `claude.yaml`'s `session_scope` block is on, the same documents scope what each session loads, over the user's and the repository's settings: an installed plugin is on only when the session preloads one of its skills, so the orch and the board session get the `ly` plugin in every hub and a writer does not, claude.ai connectors, Claude Code's bundled skills and the block's `skills_off` list are off, a plugin that is on keeps only its preloaded skills but for `ly`, which keeps all of them, and every session but the orch's gets no auto-memory and no `ListAgents` tool. The engine sets the prompt-cache TTL per role on launch and resume from `claude.yaml`'s `prompt_cache_ttl` and `prompt_cache_ttl_roles` (template: 5 minutes for `driver`, 1 hour for `webster` and `orch`, 5 minutes for every other role). `claude.yaml`'s `keep_warm_cap` (template 250s) and `keep_warm_cap_roles` set the per-role keep-warm cap beside them. While `claude.yaml`'s `batching_nudge` block is on, a `PostToolUse` hook after each `Read`, `Grep`, `Glob`, `Bash`, `Edit` or `Write` call runs the hidden `lym shuttle hook-batching`, which adds one line naming the breach and quoting the rule to a session above the block's context threshold whose recent messages make a chain of single lookups (a Bash call with a read-only lookup command such as `grep`, `find`